{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789502116,"nonce":"08NOllnkVB6sjZnTwc2B9PLTonE=","title":"The speed harness program compiles in 100 ms: dynamic attribute reads in the optparse shim","message":"Running a speed-center kernel through the harness compiles the kernel, util and the optparse shim: 92 to 117 ms on the M1 Pro before the first iteration, against 10 to 13 ms for the bench kernels. `optparse$OptionParser$parse_args` alone lowers in 8.8 ms (a 40-line method) and the module has 38 functions to optimise, among them a `py$getattr$\u003cname\u003e` dispatcher per attribute read through a dynamic value (`for option in self.options` gives `option` the type Object, since the list field's element type is not tracked), `py$call$startswith$1`, `py$call$replace$2`; drop_insert 13 ms, inline 14 ms, codegen 22 ms. On the speed center's own measure (iteration time) this does not count; on wall time it is 10% of a --fast run.\n\nTwo leads: a list field assigned an empty literal and appended instances of one class could carry that element type (then `option.name` is a struct field read); and 8.8 ms to lower one method means something in the lowering scales with the number of dynamic attribute sites times classes.","files":null}]}